  [1mMigrating the login form[0m

  [38;5;153mindex.html:9-10[39m gains [38;5;153mname="username"[39m / [38;5;153mname="password"[39m (keeping the existing ids), and the script tag becomes
  [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m. In [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m is deleted and the submit handler collapses to a schema plus
  [38;5;153mattachValidation(form, loginSchema, ({username, password}) => login(username, password))[39m. [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m gets [38;5;153m"type": [39m
  [38;5;153m"module"[39m, and [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m / [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m convert to [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m as agreed — those two files are otherwise
  untouched.

  Note this is a behavior change, deliberately: login errors currently go only to [38;5;153mconsole.error[39m, so a user gets no
  feedback at all. After this they appear next to the field. I'm treating that as the point of the exercise, but
  flagging it since it's visible to users rather than purely internal.

  [1mTesting — one snag worth your call[0m

  The pure core tests cleanly with Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m and [38;5;153mnode:assert[39m, keeping the repo at zero dependencies: [38;5;153mnpm
[39m  [38;5;153mtest[39m → [38;5;153mnode --test[39m.

  The binder is the problem. It's the layer with the fiddliest behavior — DOM insertion, clearing stale state, ARIA
  attributes — and testing it in Node needs a DOM, which means adding [38;5;153mjsdom[39m as a devDependency to a repo that currently
  has none. The honest options are to accept one devDependency and test the layer properly, or keep zero deps and leave
  the binder covered only by manual browser checks.
